vectorwake (criccomini, push to main): Subspace-inspired MMO with own art. Deterministic C99 core, Rust authoritative server, Defold client; bots emit inputs only, damage-weighted Elo. Built through M5.5.                                                                                   
The client grew multiplayer, call signs and map files, and a silent build failure shipped three stale artifacts. Zones now own their rules and send them over the wire: one weapon model, a tech tree of levels and add-ons and charges, bounty derived from what you hold.                    
Matched the original wherever it actually specifies something (prize weights, multifire cost, weapon clocks, repel and burst), but it ships no per-ship balance, so the roster is ours. Mobile keeps the pointing stick and no reverse. Hosting is Vultr; cost is egress.                      
The fleet design settled, the roster took the original's exact numbers, the game shipped to one Vultr box, and then playing it found every bug a health check could not.                                                                                                                       
The fleet went from quietly broken to a game worth looking at: live failures swept (bundle, sockets, ladder, roster, spawn kit, bots), offline mode gone, then bots live, detailed hulls, converted maps, synthesised sound, the new interface, feathered strokes, banking hulls.              
2026-08-04, the client's biggest day: living asteroids, map view, wake home screen, anchored radar, wall-wise bots, the Safari saga end to end, explosions and rung colors and field doors, kill feed authoritative, teams designed, alpha at 10 numeric teams.                                
Three days making the game legible: the field clear of hit boxes, strangers' fire heard off snapshot counts, shrapnel bouncing, an interface that explains itself: drawn keyboard, rail-and-stage menu, hover and hold-H names, a death card, corner glyphs. Metrics behind a password.        
Client polish: icons and marks measured against themselves, one helmet for pilots and bots, remote ships smoothed rather than extrapolated, snapshot-swallowed kills drawn, one key per job with multifire on backquote, a generic confirm card in the menu, bots unstick by nav.refuge.       
Two weeks on the client and the bots: one clock per rig, landmark routing, the helmet mark, zoom floored on small windows, touch controls rebuilt with safe areas and a held loadout, rungs made legible by colour and sound, spectating shipped, account keys enterable.                      
2026-08-09 Balance became measurable and the client became a phone app: a calibration harness priced the tree and fixed the prox fuse, the HUD and menu were pared for glass, audio got new voices and crossfading tracks, and the wordmark and pilot mark were drawn.                         
